{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Breakouts: The model consistently fails to anticipate sharp upward price spikes (e.g., the rise from ~82 to ~93), forecasting a flat or slightly upward trend while actual prices surge. Correction: Apply a positive momentum multiplier when the 3-day price change exceeds 5% or when sentiment history shows a cluster of positive signals (0.20+).",
    "Excessive Lag in Trend Reversal: Forecasts exhibit a significant lag, often predicting the previous day's price or a linear extrapolation of the immediate past rather than the new trend direction. Correction: Reduce the weight of the long-term moving average in favor of the most recent 3-day trend slope.",
    "Failure to Capture Volatility Expansion: The model forecasts a narrow, stable range (e.g., 84-86) regardless of the true outcome's volatility (e.g., 81-100). Correction: Increase forecast variance and introduce a volatility scaling factor proportional to the standard deviation of the last 10 days.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: Positive sentiment scores (0.20, 0.33) in the history do not correlate with upward forecast adjustments. Correction: Directly map sentiment history to a bias adjustment; a cumulative sentiment score > 0.4 over the last 5 days should trigger a minimum 3-5% upward bias in the 30-day forecast.",
    "Mean Reversion Overshoot: After the initial spike, the model fails to capture the subsequent consolidation at higher levels, often reverting too quickly to the pre-spike mean. Correction: Implement a 'sticky' mean reversion where the target mean shifts upward gradually rather than snapping back to the historical average."
  ]
}
